course: dictionary
lesson: 6302
title: More, most and more than
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: More means a bigger number or amount, or extra things coming. «Breathed-out air contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide than breathed-in air.» Here more compares two amounts: the breathed-out air has a bigger amount of carbon dioxide.
explain: «A newborn baby has more bones than an adult.» Count the bones in each and the baby has the bigger number. More also helps us compare how big or how beautiful things are, as in «more beautiful». Most is the biggest of all, so the most is the top amount.
explain: Some phrases with more mean something different. «More or less» means about, «once more» means one more time, and «no more» means none left. Check by swapping your meaning into the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have it.
fact: More means a bigger number or amount: more apples, more water.
fact: «More than» compares two things: 8 is more than 5 (8 > 5).
fact: «More» makes longer adjectives compare, as in «more beautiful».
fact: «Most» means the biggest number or amount of all.
fact: «More or less» means about; «no more» means none left or not any longer.
exercise: «A newborn baby has more bones than an adult.» What does more compare? | The size of the adult's hands | The colour of the baby's skin | How old the baby is | *The number of bones in a baby and in an adult
exercise: Lisa has 5 stickers and gets 3 more. How many does she have now? | 53 | *8 | 15 | 2
exercise: Which sign means «more than»? | = | *> | < | +
test: What does «once more» mean? | About the same amount | Lots of times at once | Not any longer | *One more time
test: What does «no more» mean in «There is no more juice left»? | *None left | Extra juice is coming | Once again | About a little
exam: A pupil is asked: by how many does 9 exceed 6? What is the answer? | *3 | 15 | 0 | 54
